WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.334
Here's a Python list of five numbers.

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:05.220
Insert 10 at index two.

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:07.089
What actually moves?

00:00:07.089 --> 00:00:10.964
A Python list doesn't store the numbers themselves.

00:00:10.964 --> 00:00:15.370
Each slot holds a reference, an arrow, to a number object.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.215
Inserting shifts the references one slot along.

00:00:19.215 --> 00:00:22.264
The objects stay exactly where they were.

00:00:22.264 --> 00:00:26.280
Their ID numbers prove it: the same objects as before.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:34.970
Languages like C and Java usually store numbers in a compact array instead, with the bits of each number right in its slot.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:36.190
That saves memory.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:42.883
But in both kinds of array, inserting at the front means moving every element.

00:00:42.883 --> 00:00:46.380
Today we build a sequence where it doesn't.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:51.727
This is Lecture 10, part three: linked lists.

00:00:52.077 --> 00:01:00.376
We'll build a list out of nodes and arrows, add and remove at its ends, and see why the order you change the arrows in is everything.

00:01:00.376 --> 00:01:06.117
Then we'll rebuild the stack and the queue on it, and meet circular and doubly linked lists.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.027
A singly linked list is a chain of nodes.

00:01:10.027 --> 00:01:15.900
Each node stores two references: one to an element, and one to the next node.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.371
The first node is the head.

00:01:18.371 --> 00:01:22.690
The last node is the tail, and the tail's next is None.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:29.400
In Python, a node is a tiny class with two attributes: element and next.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:34.640
Let's build this list by hand, starting from the tail.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.172
BOS has no next node: its next is None.

00:01:38.172 --> 00:01:39.802
ATL points to BOS.

00:01:39.802 --> 00:01:42.790
And the head, MSP, points to ATL.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.199
Head.element is MSP.

00:01:45.199 --> 00:01:47.772
Head.next.element is ATL.

00:01:47.772 --> 00:01:52.300
Follow next three times, and you reach None.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.468
Notice that making the ATL node doesn't copy BOS.

00:01:56.468 --> 00:02:00.131
It just stores one more arrow to the same node.

00:02:00.131 --> 00:02:04.650
A linked list is nothing but nodes pointing at each other.

00:02:05.434 --> 00:02:11.142
To visit every node, start at the head, and keep following next until you reach None.

00:02:11.142 --> 00:02:14.634
This is called traversing the list, or link hopping.

00:02:14.984 --> 00:02:18.012
The variable node starts at the head.

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:20.304
Print its element, then hop.

00:02:20.654 --> 00:02:21.810
Print, hop.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:22.966
Print, hop.

00:02:22.966 --> 00:02:26.854
Now node is None, and the loop stops.

00:02:27.204 --> 00:02:28.983
There's no indexing here.

00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:33.112
To reach the third node, you must hop twice from the head.

00:02:33.112 --> 00:02:35.604
A Python list jumps straight there.

00:02:36.401 --> 00:02:43.681
Now we wrap the nodes in a class that remembers the head, the tail and the size, and give it three operations.

00:02:44.031 --> 00:02:46.399
First, add at the head.

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:48.871
Make a new node for LAX.

00:02:49.221 --> 00:02:51.701
Point its next at the old head.

00:02:52.051 --> 00:02:57.211
Then make head refer to the new node, and add one to the size.

00:02:57.561 --> 00:03:01.094
If the list was empty, the new node is also the tail.

00:03:01.094 --> 00:03:03.961
Four short steps, however long the list is.

00:03:04.734 --> 00:03:11.294
To add at the tail, the old tail's next points to the new node, and then tail moves to it.

00:03:11.644 --> 00:03:15.380
But on an empty list, there is no old tail.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.855
Tail is None, and None.next is an error.

00:03:18.855 --> 00:03:22.677
The slides' pseudocode has exactly this bug.

00:03:22.677 --> 00:03:28.324
So when the list is empty, the new node becomes the head as well.

00:03:28.924 --> 00:03:34.204
To remove the head, save its element, and move head to the second node.

00:03:34.554 --> 00:03:39.169
Nothing refers to the old first node any more, so Python reclaims it.

00:03:39.169 --> 00:03:42.914
And if the list is now empty, tail must become None too.

00:03:43.264 --> 00:03:49.464
Every linked list method needs one question: what if the list is empty?

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:52.740
Pause the video and predict.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.605
Start from an empty linked list.

00:03:55.605 --> 00:04:01.244
Add first 1, add last 2, add first 3, remove first, add last 4.

00:04:01.244 --> 00:04:06.794
What are the elements now? And what are the head and the tail?

00:04:07.344 --> 00:04:08.384
Add first 1.

00:04:08.384 --> 00:04:13.064
The list was empty, so the node is both head and tail.

00:04:13.414 --> 00:04:16.405
Adding 2 at the end puts it after 1.

00:04:16.405 --> 00:04:19.894
Adding 3 at the front puts it before them.

00:04:20.244 --> 00:04:22.802
Remove first returns 3.

00:04:22.802 --> 00:04:25.804
Add last 4 goes on the end.

00:04:26.154 --> 00:04:29.451
The elements are 1, 2, 4.

00:04:29.451 --> 00:04:34.594
The head holds 1, and the tail holds 4.

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:40.998
In the add first method, the new node must take the old head before head moves.

00:04:40.998 --> 00:04:43.207
What if you swap the two lines?

00:04:43.557 --> 00:04:46.798
Head now refers to the new node.

00:04:46.798 --> 00:04:49.837
The only arrow to MSP is gone.

00:04:50.187 --> 00:04:55.587
Then the new node's next is set to head, which is the new node itself.

00:04:55.937 --> 00:05:02.166
The node points at itself, MSP is lost, and a traversal would loop forever.

00:05:02.166 --> 00:05:11.137
Draw the boxes and arrows, and change them in an order that never leaves a node with nothing pointing to it.

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:16.495
Adding at either end and removing at the head each take a few steps.

00:05:16.495 --> 00:05:18.574
Removing the tail is different.

00:05:18.924 --> 00:05:23.662
After removing it, tail must refer to the node before it.

00:05:23.662 --> 00:05:26.737
And no node knows the node before it.

00:05:26.737 --> 00:05:31.724
The only way to find it is to hop all the way from the head.

00:05:32.501 --> 00:05:38.221
The stack and queue ADTs say nothing about storage, so we can store them in nodes.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.597
A linked stack: the top is the head.

00:05:41.597 --> 00:05:45.211
Push is add first, and pop is remove first.

00:05:45.561 --> 00:05:47.288
Push is a single line.

00:05:47.288 --> 00:05:54.041
The right hand side makes the new node, pointing at the old top, before _head changes.

00:05:54.391 --> 00:05:58.946
A linked queue: the front is the head, where removing is fast.

00:05:58.946 --> 00:06:02.106
The back is the tail, where adding is fast.

00:06:02.106 --> 00:06:04.751
The queue never removes at the tail.

00:06:05.101 --> 00:06:10.705
And unlike the list queue from part two, it has no capacity.

00:06:10.705 --> 00:06:13.788
Every enqueue makes one new node.

00:06:13.788 --> 00:06:22.661
The price is memory: each element costs a node, with an element reference and a next reference.

00:06:23.434 --> 00:06:28.294
In a circularly linked list, the tail's next points back to the head.

00:06:28.294 --> 00:06:29.350
There's no end.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:34.914
The list only needs to remember the tail, because the head is always tail.next.

00:06:35.264 --> 00:06:38.457
A circle is what a round robin scheduler needs.

00:06:38.457 --> 00:06:48.104
An operating system gives each running program a short slice of time, then moves to the next, and after the last, starts again with the first.

00:06:48.454 --> 00:06:55.134
With any queue, round robin is three steps: dequeue a client, serve it, and enqueue it again.

00:06:55.484 --> 00:07:04.684
Enqueue on the circular queue: the new node points at the head, the old tail points at the new node, and the new node becomes the tail.

00:07:05.034 --> 00:07:11.194
Three programs, five turns: browser, editor, music, browser, editor.

00:07:11.968 --> 00:07:16.850
To remove the tail quickly, a node must know the node before it.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:23.488
In a doubly linked list, every node keeps two references: next, and prev, for previous.

00:07:23.838 --> 00:07:34.879
And to avoid special cases at the ends, the list has two extra nodes that hold no element: a header before the first real node, and a trailer after the last.

00:07:34.879 --> 00:07:36.638
They're called sentinels.

00:07:36.988 --> 00:07:41.655
So every real node always has a node on both sides.

00:07:41.655 --> 00:07:47.788
One method can insert anywhere, and one method can delete any node.

00:07:48.388 --> 00:07:50.991
Insert between changes four links.

00:07:50.991 --> 00:07:54.971
The new node's prev and next are set when it's made.

00:07:54.971 --> 00:07:59.948
Then the predecessor's next and the successor's prev point to it.

00:08:00.298 --> 00:08:02.189
Delete node changes two.

00:08:02.189 --> 00:08:05.498
The neighbours simply point past the node.

00:08:05.848 --> 00:08:13.049
The program from the slides inserts 10, 20 and 30, then deletes the second real node.

00:08:13.049 --> 00:08:16.608
The elements go from 10, 20, 30 to 10, 30.

00:08:16.958 --> 00:08:22.518
And removing the last real node takes no hopping at all: it's trailer.prev.

00:08:23.301 --> 00:08:24.581
Let's sum up.

00:08:24.931 --> 00:08:28.986
A singly linked list is nodes with an element and a next.

00:08:28.986 --> 00:08:33.468
Adding at either end and removing at the head take a few steps.

00:08:33.468 --> 00:08:35.531
Removing the tail takes hops.

00:08:35.881 --> 00:08:41.521
Change the arrows in a safe order, and always ask: what if the list is empty?

00:08:41.871 --> 00:08:44.108
A circular list suits round robin.

00:08:44.108 --> 00:08:50.031
A doubly linked list with sentinels inserts and deletes anywhere, with four links and two.

00:08:50.381 --> 00:08:51.826
Here's one to try.

00:08:51.826 --> 00:08:55.279
Write remove last for a singly linked list.

00:08:55.279 --> 00:08:59.053
Handle the empty list and the one element list.

00:08:59.053 --> 00:09:01.221
How many hops does it take?

00:09:01.771 --> 00:09:04.235
In part 4, we'll sort a linked list,

00:09:04.235 --> 00:09:07.451
and then leave straight lines behind for trees.

